{"subjectId":"python","id":"python-for-dsa","title":"Python for DSA & Coding Rounds","group":"Interview Prep","order":24,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The complexities, templates and tricks that make Python the fastest language to <em>write</em> correct interview solutions in.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Complexity cheat sheet</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>list</th><th>dict / set</th><th>deque</th><th>heapq</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Access by index / key</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(1) avg</td><td>O(n) in the middle</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Search <code>x in …</code></td><td>O(n)</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong> avg</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Append / add at end</td><td>O(1)*</td><td>O(1) avg</td><td>O(1)</td><td>push O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td>Remove at front</td><td><strong>O(n)</strong></td><td>—</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>pop min O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td>Insert / delete in middle</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1) avg (by key)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Min / max</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong> peek</td></tr><tr><td>Sort</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>* amortised. Strings are immutable: slicing and <code>+</code> copy — O(k).</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>Templates</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python — BFS</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\ndef bfs(graph, start):\n    \"\"\"Shortest path (edges) from start in an unweighted graph.\"\"\"\n    dist = {start: 0}\n    q = deque([start])\n    while q:\n        node = q.popleft()\n        for nxt in graph[node]:\n            if nxt not in dist:\n                dist[nxt] = dist[node] + 1\n                q.append(nxt)\n    return dist\n\ngraph = {\"a\": [\"b\", \"c\"], \"b\": [\"d\"], \"c\": [\"d\"], \"d\": []}\nprint(bfs(graph, \"a\"))          # {'a': 0, 'b': 1, 'c': 1, 'd': 2}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python — binary search</figcaption><pre><code>def lower_bound(nums, target):\n    \"\"\"First index with nums[i] &gt;= target (len(nums) if none).\"\"\"\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums)\n    while lo &lt; hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if nums[mid] &lt; target:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return lo\n\nprint(lower_bound([1, 3, 5, 7], 5), lower_bound([1, 3], 9))   # 2 2</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python — sliding window</figcaption><pre><code>def longest_unique_substring(s):\n    seen, left, best = {}, 0, 0\n    for right, ch in enumerate(s):\n        if seen.get(ch, -1) &gt;= left:\n            left = seen[ch] + 1          # shrink past the duplicate\n        seen[ch] = right\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\n\nprint(longest_unique_substring(\"abcabcbb\"))   # 3</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python — Dijkstra</figcaption><pre><code>import heapq\n\ndef dijkstra(adj, src):\n    dist = {src: 0}\n    heap = [(0, src)]\n    while heap:\n        d, u = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if d &gt; dist.get(u, float(\"inf\")):\n            continue                      # stale entry\n        for v, w in adj.get(u, []):\n            nd = d + w\n            if nd &lt; dist.get(v, float(\"inf\")):\n                dist[v] = nd\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (nd, v))\n    return dist\n\nadj = {\"a\": [(\"b\", 4), (\"c\", 1)], \"c\": [(\"b\", 2)]}\nprint(dijkstra(adj, \"a\"))                 # {'a': 0, 'b': 3, 'c': 1}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Tricks that save time</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-infinity\"></i>Infinity</h3><p><code>float('inf')</code> and <code>-float('inf')</code> for initial min/max; <code>math.inf</code> works too.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-number\"></i>Big integers</h3><p><code>int</code> never overflows — factorials and large sums need no special handling.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-keyboard\"></i>Fast input</h3><p><code>import sys; input = sys.stdin.readline</code> for large competitive-programming inputs.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>Deep recursion</h3><p><code>sys.setrecursionlimit(10**6)</code> — or better, rewrite DFS with an explicit stack.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Memoize</h3><p><code>@functools.cache</code> on a recursive function = instant top-down DP.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-grid-dots\"></i>Grids</h3><p>Direction list <code>[(0,1),(1,0),(0,-1),(-1,0)]</code> and a bounds check <code>0 &lt;= r &lt; R</code>.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import cache\n\n@cache\ndef climb(n):                 # ways to climb n stairs taking 1 or 2 steps\n    return 1 if n &lt;= 1 else climb(n - 1) + climb(n - 2)\n\nprint(climb(50))              # 20365011074</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Plain Python is roughly 10–100× slower than C++ for tight loops. Pick the right algorithm first; then lean on built-ins (<code>sum</code>, <code>sorted</code>, <code>set</code>), which run in C.</p></div></section>"}